{
  "id": "macro_ridge",
  "version": 1,
  "title": "Economic report and ML",
  "description": "Long one contract while a walk-forward ridge regression predicts the next 5 sessions' return above 1%; flat otherwise. Its features are the 5-session return and RSI 14, with the growth figure and tone a model extracted from monthly economic reports, each as published by the session's close. It refits every 20 sessions on every target known so far.",
  "required_bar": "1D",
  "conditions": [
    "Reports move the market: each model learns how the latest released growth figure and its tone, with recent price action, relate to the next 5 sessions' return.",
    "Vintages, not final figures: at each close it uses the latest month's report published by then, in its latest version by then, so a revision counts only from its own publication. Figures older than 45 days are missing. It computes no surprise, which would need the consensus forecast dated before each release.",
    "Reports are read by a model, recorded once and replayed: each figure counts from its report's publication plus a declared 5-minute processing latency. The fixture's reports are invented, so its runs are synthetic.",
    "Costs: it enters only when the predicted 5-session return exceeds 1% (0.01). A round trip costs two $2.50 commissions and two ticks of slippage ($25), $30 in all, under 0.7% of a contract's value ($50 \u00d7 price) at the fixture's prices.",
    "Chronological training: a model fitted at a session's close trains only on rows whose 5-session target is known by then, with means and scales from those rows only.",
    "Long or flat only; it never sells short.",
    "Daily bars. Signals are decided at the close and filled at the next session's open, with the backtest's commission and slippage."
  ],
